            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0398" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg388"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5–2045: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-20T13:00:00+07:00">May 20, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 20—7:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>811. If the resolutions mentioned in Embassy’s, 806, May 19, are effectively
                    implemented, thereby divorcing the army and student bosses [?] from Kuomintang
                    domination and providing for popular self-government, a most important step will
                    of course have been taken toward the achievement of democracy in China.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
